{
  "result": "I've always thought that mornings don't really begin when your alarm goes off. They begin a little earlier, in that thin, blue moment before light has fully decided to arrive. The air is cool and undecided. The world holds its breath. Cars haven't started complaining yet. Birds are still warming up their voices, testing a note or two just to see if anyone is listening.\n\nThere is a strange kindness in that hour. It doesn't ask anything from you. You don't have to be productive or interesting or even awake. You can just sit with your coffee, watch the steam curl and vanish, and be a person before you have to become an employee, a parent, a student, or a friend. It's a pause that the day gives you for free, though most of us sleep through it entirely and never collect.\n\nI think libraries understand this feeling best. They are built out of that same early-morning patience. No place else so gently insists that you are welcome without needing to buy anything, prove anything, or hurry. The books stand shoulder to shoulder like a quiet crowd, each one containing a life someone else lived, a question someone else asked. And the dust motes floating in the sun through the window - they look like tiny galaxies. If you stand still long enough, you can almost feel time soften around the edges, as if the building itself is remembering for you. And perhaps that is more than enough for today.",
